WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.859 --> 00:00:08.120
I received a ton of emails about this, one
of the most common requests for segments I've

00:00:08.120 --> 00:00:13.599
gotten over the last couple of weeks is from
people who write in and say some version of

00:00:13.599 --> 00:00:19.860
I have a Trump conspiracy theorist, family
member or friend or coworker.

00:00:19.860 --> 00:00:21.520
I don't know how to talk to them.

00:00:21.520 --> 00:00:25.270
I don't want to make anybody angry.

00:00:25.270 --> 00:00:31.350
But in some way, I feel the need to try to
knock some sense into them proverbially, not

00:00:31.350 --> 00:00:32.350
physically.

00:00:32.350 --> 00:00:33.910
And I don't even know where to start.

00:00:33.910 --> 00:00:39.600
And in the past, depending on the relationship
with the person, I say, well, you have to

00:00:39.600 --> 00:00:40.600
decide.

00:00:40.600 --> 00:00:43.760
Your approach will depend on how aggressive
you're willing to get.

00:00:43.760 --> 00:00:46.000
Are you willing to ruin this relationship?

00:00:46.000 --> 00:00:49.540
If these are presumably people, you have to
continue to be around cordially because they're

00:00:49.540 --> 00:00:54.600
coworkers or family members or friends that
you love and want to continue being around.

00:00:54.600 --> 00:01:01.380
I believe the best approach is a sort of gentle,
probing Socratic method of questioning, asking

00:01:01.380 --> 00:01:05.260
people to think about how they came to the
conclusions they came to.

00:01:05.260 --> 00:01:10.430
And hopefully some fraction of them will say,
oh, the basis on which I've come to hold these

00:01:10.430 --> 00:01:11.850
beliefs isn't that strong.

00:01:11.850 --> 00:01:13.560
Let me let me sort of reexamine it.

00:01:13.560 --> 00:01:16.630
That's the the best I've suggested we can
really hope for.

00:01:16.630 --> 00:01:22.869
If you want to go with the gentle probing
idea, Charlie Wortzel wrote a really great

00:01:22.869 --> 00:01:28.320
opinion piece in The New York Times called
How to Talk to Friends and Family who share

00:01:28.320 --> 00:01:31.979
Conspiracy Theories, which contains a lot
of really important information.

00:01:31.979 --> 00:01:34.060
And I thought we would go over some of the
basics.

00:01:34.060 --> 00:01:38.971
And I recognize that when when I've talked
about a Socratic method of questioning, one

00:01:38.971 --> 00:01:42.970
of the questions that comes up regularly is
what exactly are the types of questions that

00:01:42.970 --> 00:01:44.030
I should be asking?

00:01:44.030 --> 00:01:45.620
So let's start there.

00:01:45.620 --> 00:01:52.110
First of all, almost exclusively, the best
place to start when someone shares a conspiracy

00:01:52.110 --> 00:01:57.680
theory on Facebook or in person at Thanksgiving
or whatever the case may be, is where is the

00:01:57.680 --> 00:01:58.890
information coming from?

00:01:58.890 --> 00:02:00.630
Where did you where did you learn that?

00:02:00.630 --> 00:02:03.860
How did you come to believe that thing that
you just told me?

00:02:03.860 --> 00:02:09.761
This is useful because it opens the door to
later asking, and how do you know that you

00:02:09.761 --> 00:02:11.530
can trust that source?

00:02:11.530 --> 00:02:13.819
How do you know the information is trustworthy?

00:02:13.819 --> 00:02:15.760
So it's useful for that reason.

00:02:15.760 --> 00:02:20.680
It's also useful because it helps you understand
the sort of information environment.

00:02:20.680 --> 00:02:22.489
Where are some people getting this stuff?

00:02:22.489 --> 00:02:25.739
Some might be getting it from Facebook, others
may be hearing it at work.

00:02:25.739 --> 00:02:27.560
So that's very important.

00:02:27.560 --> 00:02:32.790
The next step, as I sort of alluded to, would
be asking people how they've determined that

00:02:32.790 --> 00:02:34.430
the information is trustworthy.

00:02:34.430 --> 00:02:39.400
How have they determined that their source
of information can be believed?

00:02:39.400 --> 00:02:41.360
What about it makes it believable?

00:02:41.360 --> 00:02:44.100
How would they know if it were not believable?

00:02:44.100 --> 00:02:49.390
These are generally relatively inoffensive
questions that you can ask with a genuine

00:02:49.390 --> 00:02:52.709
curiosity to establish their system of belief.

00:02:52.709 --> 00:02:54.270
And they can get people to start.

00:02:54.270 --> 00:02:56.850
They can get the the gears moving.

00:02:56.850 --> 00:03:00.150
How do you identify something that would be
less believable?

00:03:00.150 --> 00:03:06.099
And a lot of this is not necessarily for you
to understand their level of media literacy,

00:03:06.099 --> 00:03:07.110
although that is useful.

00:03:07.110 --> 00:03:13.860
But again, inject the idea that they need
a process for thinking about have I properly

00:03:13.860 --> 00:03:18.459
vetted and evaluated the conspiracy or the
fake news that I'm sharing right now?

00:03:18.459 --> 00:03:23.680
And then maybe the hardest part is to really
follow through on the assumption that if they're

00:03:23.680 --> 00:03:28.400
a conspiracy theory was real, then what other
things would have to be real?

00:03:28.400 --> 00:03:31.330
And this one requires a little more knowledge
and nuance.

00:03:31.330 --> 00:03:34.690
Imagine that you're right about this conspiracy
theory.

00:03:34.690 --> 00:03:37.700
Imagine that what you're saying is true.

00:03:37.700 --> 00:03:39.069
What would happen next?

00:03:39.069 --> 00:03:42.940
What are you anticipating it would mean in
terms of this or of that?

00:03:42.940 --> 00:03:44.300
And there's a really good example.

00:03:44.300 --> 00:03:46.260
I'll try to this is on my Twitter.

00:03:46.260 --> 00:03:48.700
I'll try to re share it if I can find it.

00:03:48.700 --> 00:03:53.370
I retweeted a really good Twitter thread that
I went over last week on a stream where there

00:03:53.370 --> 00:03:57.340
was like a Hollywood screenwriter talking
to a conspiracy theorist woman back and forth

00:03:57.340 --> 00:03:58.390
on Twitter.

00:03:58.390 --> 00:03:59.470
And he actually did this.

00:03:59.470 --> 00:04:04.310
He engaged with her ideas, which started with
there's a plan for Trump to remain president,

00:04:04.310 --> 00:04:06.459
that she was posting this on January 21st.

00:04:06.459 --> 00:04:10.420
Obviously, Trump was gone, but she was saying
there is a plan for Trump to remain president

00:04:10.420 --> 00:04:12.349
after the inauguration.

00:04:12.349 --> 00:04:17.939
And this guy started asking specific questions,
OK, how will it be carried out?

00:04:17.939 --> 00:04:22.979
Through what mechanism will Trump be back
in office and Biden be gone?

00:04:22.979 --> 00:04:27.159
Well, he's going to be arrested, OK, what
will he be arrested for?

00:04:27.159 --> 00:04:32.569
And eventually, the woman came to the claim
that Joe Biden had actually already died and

00:04:32.569 --> 00:04:35.669
been replaced with an imposter at some point
in the past.

00:04:35.669 --> 00:04:40.620
And it was fascinating to see the questions
that followed, which included, oh, how did

00:04:40.620 --> 00:04:41.620
Biden die?

00:04:41.620 --> 00:04:45.509
Was it of natural causes disease or did somebody
kill him?

00:04:45.509 --> 00:04:47.030
If he was killed, who killed him?

00:04:47.030 --> 00:04:48.030
How is it covered up?

00:04:48.030 --> 00:04:51.689
How many people would have needed to be involved
in that to replace Biden with an imposter,

00:04:51.689 --> 00:04:53.000
with nobody knowing?

00:04:53.000 --> 00:04:54.669
Why did they even need to kill him?

00:04:54.669 --> 00:04:57.600
Like, in other words, if Biden was going to
steal the election?

00:04:57.600 --> 00:05:00.270
Either way, why do you need a fake Biden to
steal the.

00:05:00.270 --> 00:05:05.189
From Donald Trump, why not just have the real
Biden steal the election from Donald Trump

00:05:05.189 --> 00:05:10.939
and although this particular woman was clearly
too far beyond reach, what was interesting

00:05:10.939 --> 00:05:14.960
was that it was a really good example of the
types of questions that for some people can

00:05:14.960 --> 00:05:17.020
actually get them to say.

00:05:17.020 --> 00:05:19.379
Maybe there's something about this that doesn't
make sense.

00:05:19.379 --> 00:05:20.379
Really important.

00:05:20.379 --> 00:05:24.809
Last thought on this, just because a belief
isn't based on a solid foundation doesn't

00:05:24.809 --> 00:05:26.909
mean people will give it up easily.

00:05:26.909 --> 00:05:32.889
And remember that just because this idea,
this system doesn't work on one person doesn't

00:05:32.889 --> 00:05:34.639
mean that it's the wrong method.

00:05:34.639 --> 00:05:38.379
We looked at that focus group with 12 Trump
last week.

00:05:38.379 --> 00:05:42.529
Only one of the 12 was willing to reexamine
his beliefs and see reason.

00:05:42.529 --> 00:05:47.210
So this a lot of people aren't even open to
this.

00:05:47.210 --> 00:05:52.580
But for those that are, I find that this method
seems to be the best one.

00:05:52.580 --> 00:05:53.810
Let me know your thoughts.

00:05:53.810 --> 00:05:58.979
If you've had success with this method, let
me know if you've failed with this method.

00:05:58.979 --> 00:05:59.979
Also, let me know.

00:05:59.979 --> 00:06:50.490
I'm on Twitter at Dipak The David Pakman Show
at David 

00:06:50.490 --> 00:07:03.199
Pakman 

00:07:03.199 --> 00:07:03.829
dot com

